Revision history for Proxy-Impersonate

0.03  2026-07-16
    - Chrome navigations now carry Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1. Real Chrome sends
      it on every navigation (not user-gesture-gated) but WebKit never emits it, so
      it is synthesized per target + destination: a Chrome-target document / iframe
      / frame request gets it; subresources and Safari targets do not (matching each
      real browser). Previously it was stripped like a user-gated header -- a
      conspicuous gap next to an otherwise-perfect Chrome request.
    - A CONNECT arriving while pre-TLS plaintext is still queued (a prior pipelined
      response to a slow client) defers the TLS handshake until that has flushed, so
      the 200 is not stranded behind it by the handshake state. Bytes a client sends
      during that deferred window (before reading the 200) fast-close the connection
      rather than being stranded in rbuf (the later set_fd handshake cannot see
      them), mirroring the coalesced-ClientHello guard.
    - Weaken $self in the daemon's accept watcher: dropping the proxy reference
      without stop()/shutdown() no longer leaks it (kept alive, still accepting) via
      the watcher self-cycle. Documented usage (hold the ref, then stop/shutdown) is
      unchanged.
    - A CONNECT that coalesces an optimistic ClientHello (TLS bytes in the same
      segment as the CONNECT request, before our 200) is now closed immediately
      instead of stalling until the idle timeout: set_fd binds OpenSSL to the
      socket, so bytes already buffered in rbuf are invisible to the handshake.
      Compliant clients (browsers, libsoup) wait for the 200 and are unaffected;
      documented in POD.
    - Fix corrupted responses under backpressure: on_body appended a chunk and
      THEN returned CURL_WRITEFUNC_PAUSE, but libcurl treats a PAUSE return as
      "not consumed" and re-delivers the chunk on resume -- so it was written
      twice, duplicating a 16 KiB block in any response larger than HIWAT. Now
      pause BEFORE consuming. (Regression test t/44 streams a 2 MiB body to a slow
      client and checks it byte-for-byte.)
    - The Chrome-family flag also matches Chromium/Edge targets (they send Chrome-
      style Accept); a non-matching Chromium target can force it with chrome => 1.
    - Fix truncated HTTPS responses under backpressure: set
      SSL_MODE_ACCEPT_MOVING_WRITE_BUFFER on the connection so an SSL_write retry
      whose buffer moved (an on_body append reallocated the Perl PV between a
      WANT_WRITE and the retry) no longer fails with "bad write retry".
    - Accept is now target-family-aware: a Chrome target synthesizes Chrome's per-
      destination Accept, but a Safari target (safari18_0*) forwards WebKit's own
      (Safari-correct) Accept instead of injecting Chrome-only tokens
      (application/signed-exchange, image/avif) next to a Safari JA4/UA.
    - Cap the request head at 64 KiB (close a never-terminating / slow-drip header
      section the idle timer would not reclaim).
    - Accept map covers frame navigations (Sec-Fetch-Dest: iframe/frame get the
      Chrome document Accept, not */*); an unmapped dest that carries an app-set
      Accept (EventSource text/event-stream, fetch application/json) forwards it
      rather than flattening to */*.
    - Accept-Language is no longer forwarded from WebKit (libsoup may format it
      unlike Chrome); it is supplied as an identity header in Chrome's exact
      format by EV::WebKit instead.
    - IPv6-literal authorities ([::1]:443) are parsed correctly for the per-host
      Accept-CH hints key (was truncating to a wrong key).
    - Client idle timeout: a connection that makes no read/write progress for
      120s is closed, reclaiming a stalled handshake / partial head / a peer that
      stopped reading a paused download (was pinned with its watcher armed).
    - Coherence: forward WebKit's real per-request Sec-Fetch-* + Accept headers
      (override curl's static navigation defaults) and strip Sec-Fetch-User /
      Upgrade-Insecure-Requests when WebKit omits them, so a subresource no longer
      looks like a document load. Requires Curl::Impersonate 0.03.
    - Ignore SIGPIPE: a client resetting mid-response no longer terminates the
      (in-process) proxy before the graceful abort runs.
    - Accept is synthesized to Chrome's per-destination value from Sec-Fetch-Dest
      (was forwarding WebKit's Safari-flavored Accept -- a Chrome/JA4 request with
      a Safari Accept is a tell; curl's static document Accept was wrong for
      subresources).
    - Chunked-body 411 rejection consumes the head + latches closing (no repeated
      411s on a partial flush); tightened SSL want-read/write watcher handling.
    - The Accept-CH host map is capped to bound memory on a long-running proxy.
    - high_entropy_headers => : high-entropy client hints (Sec-CH-UA-Platform-
      Version/Arch/Bitness/Model/Full-Version-List) are added per-host only after
      that host sends Accept-CH, matching real Chrome (and the JS ua_data).
    - Multiple Set-Cookie (and other repeated response headers) are relayed as
      separate lines instead of collapsing to the last value.
    - Bodyless upstream responses (204/304/HEAD) are relayed instead of hanging.
    - Fixed a per-session leak: the curl_multi<->EV callbacks formed a reference
      cycle that prevented curl_multi_cleanup from running (weaken the closures).
    - Drain the SSL buffer after the handshake (a coalesced TLS 1.3 request could
      hang); reject chunked request bodies cleanly (411) instead of corrupting the
      next parse; a client write failure aborts the upstream via curl rather than
      a reentrant remove; fixed SSL renegotiation want-read/write handling +
      write-watcher busy-loop.
    - response_head strips control chars (response-splitting guard); the CA key is
      created 0600 via sysopen (no world-readable open->chmod window); ca_dir 0700.

0.02  2026-07-16
    - Add shutdown(): in-process teardown that closes active connections and
      releases the curl_multi wiring without EV::break, for embedding the proxy
      in another EV loop (e.g. EV::WebKit).

0.01  2026-07-16
    - Initial release.
    - EV MITM proxy: terminates a client's TLS with a single persisted
      self-signed cert and re-originates each request upstream through
      Curl::Impersonate, so origins see the chosen browser's TLS (JA3/JA4) and
      HTTP/2 (Akamai) connection fingerprint instead of the local TLS stack.
    - HTTP and HTTPS-via-CONNECT; h1/h2 upstream; incremental response streaming
      with backpressure (CURLPAUSE); keep-alive for fixed-length responses,
      close-delimiting for unknown-length ones.
    - Companion to EV::WebKit: the WebKit side accepts the proxy cert via
      set_tls_errors_policy(IGNORE) and routes through it with set_proxy (the
      SSL_CERT_FILE CA-trust path was spiked and does not work on WebKitGTK's
      GnuTLS build).
    - Not in scope this release: WebSockets, HTTP/3 upstream, streaming request
      uploads.
